	doi = {10.1037/0096-1523.19.6.1162},
	abstract = {[Correction Notice: An erratum for this article was reported in Vol 20(1) of {\textless}xh:i xmlns:search="http://marklogic.com/appservices/search" xmlns="http://apa.org/pimain" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xh="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"{\textgreater}Journal of Experimental Psychology: Human Perception and Performance{\textless}/xh:i{\textgreater} (see record {\textless}RelatedUID xmlns:search="http://marklogic.com/appservices/search" xmlns="http://apa.org/pimain" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xh="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"{\textgreater}2008-10476-001{\textless}/RelatedUID{\textgreater}). Figure 4 is in error; it appears correctly in the erratum.] Five experiments on the effects of changes of depth orientation on (1) priming the naming of briefly flashed familiar objects, (2) matching individual sample volumes (geons), and (3) classifying unfamiliar objects (that could readily be decomposed into an arrangement of distinctive geons) all revealed immediate (i.e., not requiring practice) depth variance. The results can be understood in terms of 3 conditions derived from a model of object recognition (I. Biederman, 1987; J. E. Hummel and Biederman, 1992) that have to be satisfied for immediate depth invariance: (1) that the stimuli be capable of activating viewpoint-invariant (e.g., geon) structural descriptions (GSDs), (2) that the GSDs be distinctive (different) for each stimulus, and (3) that the same GSD be activated in original and tested views. The stimuli used in several recent experiments documenting extraordinary viewpoint dependence violated these conditions. [A correction concerning this article appears in {\textless}xh:i xmlns:search="http://marklogic.com/appservices/search" xmlns="http://apa.org/pimain" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xh="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"{\textgreater}Journal of Experimental Psychology: Human Perception and Performance,{\